Actual candidates: 6; candidate row tests: 24; owned model nodes: 3.
\image(figures/c45_threshold.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Six actual candidates: three adjacent-value boundaries for each of two features. Only the first feature's midpoint at 1.5 is selected.)
\alttext(A root testing feature zero at one point five sends original rows zero and one to class zero, and rows two and three to class one. Both child entropies are zero.)

At each node the learner sorts distinct observed values per feature, considers
all adjacent boundaries, and chooses the first strictly positive maximum ratio.
An explicit work stack grows the left subtree before the right. Every split
has two nonempty children, so each path has fewer rows after a split and the
unlimited tree terminates. Majority labels use the smallest integer to break
class ties. A node without a positive computed ratio stays a majority leaf.

Gain ratio normalizes by the entropy of the partition, rather than by its number
of children. It does not universally remove attribute-selection bias. Quinlan's
account restricts selection to tests with at least average gain; the later
continuous-attribute modification also charges a threshold-description penalty
and changes threshold selection. This API retains its original unrestricted
maximum-ratio rule. All tests here are binary, and the report does not claim to
implement the full C4.5 system. See the [original paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/9603103).

> Iris: measure predictions on flowers excluded from fitting

The native iris frame contains 150 flowers, 50 per species, with four measurements
in centimetres. We use every measurement and code setosa, versicolor and virginica
as 0,1,2. Row index modulo five defines fixed folds, each containing ten flowers
per species. Every prediction in the cross-validation column comes from a model
fitted on the other 120 flowers. This deterministic fold rule depends on dataset
order; it is a reproducible illustration, not an independent external validation.
Dataset structure follows the [R iris documentation](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/iris.html).

The separate full-data training fraction measures fit on the model's own 150
rows. Looking at all six depth settings uses the same folds repeatedly; selecting
a depth from this table and treating its maximum as an unbiased final estimate
would require a further outer evaluation. There is no imputation, scaling or
feature selection. Classification uses only coordinate ordering.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/c45_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris=dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  const std::vector<std::string> species{"setosa","versicolor","virginica"};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> x(iris.nrows());std::vector<int> y;
  const auto& labels=iris.string_column("Species").values();
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<iris.nrows();++i) {
    for(const auto& name:names) x[i].push_back(iris.double_at(name,i));
    auto position=std::find(species.begin(),species.end(),labels[i]);
    if(position==species.end()) return 1;
    y.push_back(static_cast<int>(position-species.begin()));
  }
  std::vector<double> depths,training,validation;
  std::print("| depth cap | full-data nodes | training correct / 150 | five-fold correct / 150 | training fraction | cross-validation fraction |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t depth=1;depth<=6;++depth) {
    const auto tree=dm::algorithms::c45_train(x,y,depth,2);std::size_t fit_hits=0,cv_hits=0;
    for(std::size_t i=0;i<x.size();++i) fit_hits+=dm::algorithms::c45_predict(tree,x[i])==y[i];
    for(std::size_t fold=0;fold<5;++fold) {
      std::vector<std::vector<double>> train;std::vector<int> target;
      for(std::size_t i=0;i<x.size();++i) if(i%5!=fold) {train.push_back(x[i]);target.push_back(y[i]);}
      if(train.size()!=120) return 1;
      const auto model=dm::algorithms::c45_train(train,target,depth,2);
      for(std::size_t i=0;i<x.size();++i) if(i%5==fold) cv_hits+=dm::algorithms::c45_predict(model,x[i])==y[i];
    }
    depths.push_back(depth);training.push_back(static_cast<double>(fit_hits)/x.size());validation.push_back(static_cast<double>(cv_hits)/x.size());
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",depth,tree.nodes.size(),fit_hits,cv_hits,training.back(),validation.back());
  }
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace(x,y,{1,2,0});
  for(const auto& c:trace.candidates) if(c.selected)
    std::print("\nFull-data root: {} <= {:.3f}; gain {:.6f}, ratio {:.6f}; sizes {} and {}.\n",
        names[c.feature],c.threshold,c.gain,c.ratio,c.left_count,c.right_count);
  std::print("Root candidates: {}; actual candidate row tests: {}.\n",trace.candidate_count,trace.candidate_row_tests);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(depths,training,"training",{37,99,235},2.5)
      .points(depths,training,"",{37,99,235},4).line(depths,validation,"five-fold held-out",{220,38,38},2.5)
      .points(depths,validation,"",{220,38,38},4).title("Iris: training fit and five-fold predictions")
      .x_label("maximum split depth (root depth zero)").y_label("correct fraction").y_limits(0.5,1.06)
      .save_svg("build/c45_iris.svg");
}
```
