\image(figures/c45_accuracy.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original well-separated 180-point control reaches perfect training fit with two split levels. This is not a held-out generalization result.)
\alttext(Training accuracy rises from two thirds at depth one to one at depth two, then remains at one through depth six.)

> Stopping, numeric boundaries and complete-call budgets

A parent with at least min_samples rows may split off a single row. This
parameter is not a minimum leaf size. Identical feature rows with contradictory
labels cannot be separated by a threshold. Pure XOR also has zero gain at every
root test, so this greedy learner stops at a majority leaf with half the labels
correct; two levels of a manually chosen XOR tree would classify all four.

The old midpoint expression could overflow on finite values or round to the
upper boundary and discard a valid separating test. The checked implementation
uses std::midpoint and falls back to the lower observed value if necessary.
Training rejects nonfinite features, mismatched labels and ragged rows before
purity shortcuts. Prediction checks the visited path, including tested feature
availability, finite tests/values, child indices and cycles. Unvisited branches
and unused query values are deliberately not a whole-model validation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto xor_trace=dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace({{0,0},{0,1},{1,0},{1,1}},{0,1,1,0});
  if(xor_trace.nodes[0].reason!=dm::algorithms::C45StopReason::no_positive_gain) return 1;
  std::print("XOR: {} root candidates; model nodes {}; majority label {}.\n",xor_trace.candidate_count,
      xor_trace.tree.nodes.size(),xor_trace.tree.nodes[0].prediction);
  const double lo=1,hi=std::nextafter(1.0,2.0);
  const auto adjacent=dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace({{lo},{hi}},{3,7});
  std::print("Adjacent doubles: threshold {:.17g}, at lower -> {}, at upper -> {}.\n",adjacent.tree.nodes[0].threshold,
      dm::algorithms::c45_predict(adjacent.tree,{lo}),dm::algorithms::c45_predict(adjacent.tree,{hi}));
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> x{{0},{1},{2},{3}};const std::vector<int> y{0,1,0,1};
  const auto full=dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace(x,y);
  bool rejected=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace(x,y,{0,2,full.candidate_count-1});}
  catch(const std::length_error&) {rejected=true;}
  const auto exact=dm::algorithms::c45_train_trace(x,y,{0,2,full.candidate_count});
  if(!rejected || exact.tree.nodes.size()!=full.tree.nodes.size()) return 1;
  std::print("Alternating four labels: {} candidates, {} row tests, {} nodes; one-short budget rejected: {}.\n",
      full.candidate_count,full.candidate_row_tests,full.tree.nodes.size(),rejected);
  bool invalid=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::c45_train({{std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN()}},{1});}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {invalid=true;}
  bool short_query=false;try {(void)dm::algorithms::c45_predict(adjacent.tree,{});}
  catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {short_query=true;}
  if(!invalid || !short_query) return 1;
  std::print("Nonfinite singleton training rejected: {}; short visited query rejected: {}.\n",invalid,short_query);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
XOR: 2 root candidates; model nodes 1; majority label 0.
Adjacent doubles: threshold 1, at lower -> 3, at upper -> 7.
Alternating four labels: 6 candidates, 20 row tests, 7 nodes; one-short budget rejected: true.
Nonfinite singleton training rejected: true; short visited query rejected: true.
// result_end

The complete-call candidate budget includes zero-gain tests and throws before
partitioning an excess candidate. It returns no partial model. candidate_row_tests
counts only row/threshold comparisons used to evaluate candidates; it excludes
sorting, class-map operations, the final selected repartition and prediction.
Neither counter is a timing or a full operation count.

At a searched node of m rows and d features there are at most d(m-1) candidates.
This implementation repartitions all m rows and recomputes ordered-map entropies
for each, giving the upper bound O(d*m*m*log(m)) per searched node, summed over
the tree. It does not use a prefix-count threshold sweep. Ordinary training keeps
no provenance records. Tracing additionally stores node row memberships and every
actual candidate, requiring O(n*h+c) storage for reached depth h and c candidates.
All construction and ownership use flat arrays and explicit pending partitions.

Native verification compares 37,324 exhaustive small one-dimensional training
configurations and 1,000 random multifeature/multiclass cases against a separate
recursive, materialized-label trainer. Additional cases check exact scores and
ties, original cloud draw order, ownership, stopping reasons, whole-call budgets,
invalid training and visited prediction paths, extreme finite midpoints,
adjacent representable values and subnormals. The tests check the implemented
restricted learner; they do not establish equivalence to a full C4.5 release.

> References

- J. R. Quinlan (1996). [Improved Use of Continuous Attributes in C4.5](https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/9603103). Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research 4, 77-90. The paper describes gain ratio, the average-gain restriction and the later continuous-threshold correction; the latter two are not implemented here.
- R Core Team. [Edgar Anderson's Iris Data](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/iris.html). Dataset measurements, species, provenance and original references.
